WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.695
On QNX, the hardest bugs are often not logic bugs.

2
00:00:03.695 --> 00:00:10.384
They are timing bugs: a control loop that misses a deadline, or a safety thread blocked by a logger.

3
00:00:10.384 --> 00:00:13.595
They are designed in before the hardware exists.

4
00:00:13.595 --> 00:00:16.940
Scheduloscope makes that design review executable.

5
00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:26.574
The whole design is one YAML file: five threads, their priorities and periods, a mutex, a service channel, and an interrupt.

6
00:00:26.574 --> 00:00:32.350
The model uses QNX scheduling terms directly, not a generic real-time abstraction.

7
00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:36.440
One command runs response-time analysis.

8
00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:42.102
It includes blocking, interrupt interference, and context-switch costs.

9
00:00:42.102 --> 00:00:48.483
Five tasks, 68.8 percent utilization, worst slack 361 microseconds: schedulable.

10
00:00:48.483 --> 00:00:52.550
The report also names the term closest to the edge.

11
00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:56.815
The same model becomes a self-contained HTML report.

12
00:00:56.815 --> 00:01:02.897
It shows the actual simulated schedule: every context switch, critical section, and interrupt.

13
00:01:02.897 --> 00:01:08.720
The one inversion is mitigated, because the logger inherited the safety thread's priority.

14
00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:14.658
Now change one line: the mutex protocol is none instead of inherit.

15
00:01:14.658 --> 00:01:16.086
The analysis fails.

16
00:01:16.086 --> 00:01:23.530
Control misses by 538 microseconds, and the largest term is 900 microseconds of unbounded blocking.

17
00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:27.950
The simulation shows the mechanism, not just the verdict.

18
00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:33.694
The logger holds the lock, a middle-priority thread preempts it, and the safety thread waits.

19
00:01:33.694 --> 00:01:38.450
That is priority inversion, caught in a design review instead of on a target.

20
00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:52.888
The same model generates a C harness for QNX 8.0: real scheduling policies, real mutex protocols, a real channel with server threads, and ClockCycles timestamps.

21
00:01:52.888 --> 00:01:57.260
Make qnx builds x86_64; make qnx-arm builds AArch64.

22
00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:00.400
QNX runs on both targets now.

23
00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:06.900
x86_64 boots from its own BIOS IFS under KVM with host CPU.

24
00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:14.600
AArch64 boots through a public MIT-licensed BSP and Q-Boot image under TCG, and the same generated harness writes platform qnx on both.

25
00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:22.800
I ran three KVM boots and one TCG comparison; all four wrote complete reports.

26
00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:27.600
These are one-vCPU emulated guests: runtime reliability, not hardware latency.

27
00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:31.000
Official QSTI and physical-board timing remain open.

28
00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:33.550
Everything is Apache 2.0.

29
00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:35.550
One hundred and six tests.

30
00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:43.200
CI compiles the generated C with warnings as errors, and the repository says exactly what was measured and what was not.

31
00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:47.600
Scheduloscope: find the timing bug before the hardware does.
